{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3276","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-contract-verification-async-reporting.md@sha256:c2ced632324b7b3581c88eb1f73b946e8de6c915a5a9cb967b4b77589c8c33cf"},"owner_reference":"3276","content":"# Architecture Spec: Contract-Verification Async Reporting (#3276)\n\n**Scope**: instruction-content changes only (agent/skill markdown). No Python/MCP server changes,\nno new `artifact_type` enum values, no new tools. This document specifies WHAT each of the 5 files\nmust say \u2014 exact destinations, section names, input/output contracts, and call flow \u2014 not the\nprose itself.\n\n**Verified primary sources** (not just the feature-context summary):\n- `git show 11082f0eb -- plugins/development-harness/agents/contract-verification.md` \u2014 confirms\n  the commit deliberately replaced the agent's `SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", \u2026)` calls with\n  `\"Your finding is your response\u2026\"`, while leaving the `tools:` frontmatter's `SendMessage` grant\n  untouched (the diff touches only the two instruction blocks, not `tools:`). Commit message states\n  the rationale verbatim: *\"SendMessage is a Claude-Code-only tool. This plugin's shipped\n  instruction content also targets Codex, OpenCode, Crush and Kilo, where an agent has no such\n  tool, so every instruction to use it is unexecutable there.\"*\n- `git show 43058f345 -- plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` \u2014 confirms the\n  #3249 fix added `SendMessage(to=\"team-lead\", \u2026)`, and \u2014 notably \u2014 its own fallback text\n  (*\"When not operating as a teammate (no team-lead present), end your response with the same\n  `STATUS: DONE` block as your final output instead\"*) makes the **same** synchronous-return\n  assumption #3276 is fixing. This fix is confirmed non-transferable, exactly as graded.\n- `.claude/rules/agent-output-contracts.md` (governs `**/agents/**/*.md`, so it binds\n  `contract-verification.md` and `t0-baseline-capture.md`/`tn-verification-gate.md` today) states\n  the operative principle directly: *\"That send [SendMessage] is a notification, never the record.\n  Put anything a later step reads in a named destination that step can read back \u2014 a task section,\n  an artifact, a review thread.\"* This is the authoritative statement of the pattern this spec\n  applies; it is not a new invention.\n- `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__artifact_register`'s live tool schema \u2014 `artifact_type` is a closed\n  enum: `feature-context, architect, task-plan, T0-baseline, TN-verification, codebase-analysis,\n  code-review, research, dispatch-plan, audit-report`. No generic/narrative type exists. Adding one\n  is an MCP-server (Python) change \u2014 out of scope for this item. This is the deciding fact for \u00a71.\n\n---\n\n## 1. `agents/contract-verification.md`\n\n### Decision: `backlog_groom` \u2192 Concerns section, not `artifact_register`\n\n**Chosen destination**: the calling backlog item's `Concerns` section, via\n`mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom`, written by `contract-verification.md` itself before it\nreturns \u2014 not `artifact_register`, and not a new artifact type.\n\n**Justification against the two precedents:**\n\n| | `artifact_register` (T0/TN precedent) | `backlog_groom` Concerns (chosen) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fits a closed `artifact_type` enum today? | No \u2014 no type matches a narrative concerns list; adding one is a server-code change, out of scope | Yes \u2014 `backlog_groom(section=\"Concerns\")` needs no new type |\n| Caller already knows the target at dispatch time? | T0/TN key by `item_id` alone (no natural section) | `implement-feature` step 4a already has `{issue}` in scope *and* already contains the exact `backlog_groom(section=\"Concerns\", append=True)` call this fix needs \u2014 it currently just calls it on the wrong side (post-return, from data that never arrives) |\n| Matches sibling pattern in the same file? | N/A | Step 4 (regular task-agent concerns) already appends to the **same** `Concerns` section with the **same** `- [ ] {concern} (reported by {agent} on {task_id})` shape. Reusing it keeps one concerns channel per backlog item instead of two (Concerns for task agents, a separate artifact type for contract-verification alone) |\n| Consumed downstream? | TN is read back by one specific step (`/complete-implementation`'s regression gate) | Concerns is already the established multi-producer channel: *\"Concerns accumulate across all task agents. They feed into the validation stage in `/complete-implementation` \u2014 each verified concern becomes a new backlog item.\"* (`implement-feature/SKILL.md:149`) |\n\n**Justification against Design Intent Alignment's MISSION_ALIGNED verdict**: the verdict endorses\nrouting findings through the backend over `SendMessage`-to-team-lead specifically because\n`11082f0eb`'s rationale is cross-harness portability \u2014 a backend write executes identically on\nCodex/OpenCode/Crush/Kilo, a `SendMessage` call does not. `backlog_groom` satisfies this exactly\nas `artifact_register` would; the enum-fit problem is what selects between the two backend\noptions, not alignment.\n\n### Required changes\n\n**A. New required input** (`## Inputs`, currently lines 23\u201332): add `issue` \u2014 the parent backlog\nitem selector (`str | int`; GitHub issue number or beads nanoid), the same type already used\nelsewhere in this plugin (`item_id`/`selector` convention). Add it to the existing \"if any input is\nmissing\u2026 return BLOCKED immediately\" check \u2014 no new BLOCKED branch needed, just extend the\nexisting one.\n\n**B. Replace line 151.** Current text:\n\n> Your finding is your response. The dispatcher reads it there \u2014 you register no artifact and\n> write no task state.\n\nReplace with an instruction that, in both the mismatches-found and no-mismatches branches, before\nreturning:\n\n1. Calls `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_groom` with `selector=\"#{issue}\"`, `section=\"Concerns\"`,\n   `append=True`, and content built as:\n   - **One entry per concern found** (mismatches branch): `- [ ] CONTRACT: {concern text} (reported by contract-verification on {task_id})` \u2014 unchecked, matching the exact template `implement-feature/SKILL.md:193` already contains, so it is still an actionable item `/complete-implementation`'s validation stage can turn into a follow-up backlog item.\n   - **One entry when clean** (no-mismatches branch): `- [x] CONTRACT: No contract concerns \u2014 all contracts in scope are satisfied (verified by contract-verification on {task_id})` \u2014 **checked**, not omitted. This is what satisfies feature-context outcome 2 (\"clean results are distinguishable from dropped results\"): a checked box is an affirmative record of a clean run, distinct from silence, and distinct from an open (unchecked) violation that `/complete-implementation` should still act on.\n2. Only after that write succeeds, returns per `subagent-contract`'s `<status>` convention:\n   `STATUS: DONE` as the first line, followed by the same concerns block (or clean message) as\n   before \u2014 kept for two reasons: (a) `.claude/rules/agent-output-contracts.md` requires every\n   agent to emit an explicit terminal message regardless of whether anything reads it, and (b) it\n   costs nothing and remains useful in any dispatch context where a return value *is* received\n   (e.g. a future synchronous harness, or a human reading the transcript).\n\nThe distinction from the old line 151 is precise: the backend write is now the record: the\nreturned text is best-effort, never load-bearing.\n\n**C. Revoke the `SendMessage` tool grant** (frontmatter `tools:` line, currently line 5). Per the\nfeature-context's own Q6 and R4: contract-verification is dispatched as a bare, unnamed, non-team\n`Agent()` \u2014 there is no `team-lead` to address and no other addressable recipient in this dispatch\nshape, so the grant is dead capability that exists only as a temptation to reach for the\n`impact-analyst`-style fix that R4 explicitly rules out. Removing it makes the tool surface match\nwhat the instructions actually do (`agent-output-contracts.md` enforcement point 4 treats the\ngrant as a capability signal, not decoration \u2014 an unused grant is exactly the kind of drift that\nrule exists to catch).\n\n---\n\n## 2. `skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` step 4a\n\n**Current structure** (lines 151\u2013200): resolve modified files \u2192 dispatch\n`Agent(subagent_type=\"dh:contract-verification\", \u2026)` \u2192 *\"If the contract-verification agent\nreturns a `<concerns>` block, append each concern\u2026\"* (branches on a return value that never\narrives) \u2192 `backlog_groom(section=\"Concerns\", append=True)`.\n\n**New structure:**\n\n1. Dispatch prompt (lines 166\u2013184) gains one new line carrying the issue: add\n   `Backlog item: #{issue}` (or equivalent `item_id={issue}` line) alongside `task_id`, `plan`,\n   `architect spec`, `modified_files` \u2014 this is the new required input from \u00a71.A. `{issue}` is\n   already in scope at this call site (step 4a's own precondition is \"if a parent issue number is\n   known\").\n2. **Delete** the \"If the contract-verification agent returns a `<concerns>` block, append\u2026\" branch\n   (lines 187\u2013196) and its `backlog_groom` call. That write now happens *inside*\n   `contract-verification.md` itself (\u00a71.B) \u2014 the orchestrator must not also write it, or a\n   dispatch that does complete synchronously would double-append.\n3. The dispatch becomes fire-and-forget: step 4a does not block on, poll for, or branch on the\n   `Agent()` call's return value at all. This is not new latency-tolerant behavior invented for\n   this fix \u2014 it is simply no longer *pretending* to be synchronous when the runtime already isn't.\n4. **Read-back, not return-value branch**, at the one place a fresh Concerns view is actually\n   displayed: the Per-task Confirmation Gate (`autonomy_mode == \"per_task\"`, lines 254\u2013261), which\n   shows \"Any concerns raised\" before dispatching the next task. Change its concerns source from\n   \"the concerns block check in step 4\" (an in-memory value) to a fresh\n   `backlog_view(selector=\"#{issue}\", section=\"Concerns\", show=\"last\")` read, immediately before\n   rendering the summary \u2014 this picks up any `CONTRACT:` entries `contract-verification` finished\n   writing since dispatch, whether or not the dispatch itself has visibly completed.\n5. **No new completion-signal mechanism is introduced.** If contract-verification's write lands\n   after the confirmation gate for its own task has already rendered, it is still durably present\n   in Concerns and is picked up by the *next* task's confirmation-gate read, or \u2014 worst case \u2014 by\n   `/complete-implementation`'s validation stage, which re-reads the full Concerns section\n   regardless (`SKILL.md:149`). This satisfies feature-context outcome 1 (\"becomes visible\u2026 after\n   the agent finishes \u2014 every time\") without a blocking wait, consistent with R5 (no new sync\n   mechanism for a two-site problem).\n\n---\n\n## 3. `groom/error.md` Agent Failure procedure\n\n**Current structure** (lines 11\u201340): dispatch `Agent(subagent_type=\"dh:task-worker\", prompt=\"\u2026\nReport: last_success, first_failure, failure_type\")` \u2192 Mermaid tree branches directly on the\nreturned `failure_type` value. Same defect as \u00a71: `dh:task-worker` here is an ad hoc bare `Agent()`\ndispatch (not a fixed agent file with its own Output Format section), so the fix must be expressed\nin the **inline prompt string** this file constructs, plus a read-back step this file adds before\nits own Mermaid tree runs.\n\n**Destination**: the `Context` section of `<item_ref/>` (the item currently being groomed) \u2014 not\n`Concerns`. Concerns is the wrong channel here: `implement-feature/SKILL.md:149` states every\nConcerns entry is later read by `/complete-implementation`'s validation stage, where *\"each\nverified concern becomes a new backlog item.\"* A transient orchestration diagnostic\n(`failure_type=interrupted`) is not a review-worthy concern and must not spawn a follow-up backlog\nitem months later when this same item eventually reaches implementation. `Context` carries no such\ndownstream consumer and is already a standard section (confirmed present on #3276 itself via\n`backlog_view`'s `sections_index`).\n\n**Required changes:**\n\n1. **Step 3's prompt** gains an explicit final instruction, mirroring \u00a71.B's shape: after\n   determining `last_success` / `first_failure` / `failure_type`, call `backlog_groom` with\n   `selector=<item_ref/>`, `section=\"Context\"`, `append=True`, content in a fixed, greppable\n   key=value line so the read-back in step 4 (below) can parse it reliably, e.g.:\n   `DIAGNOSTIC ({ISO8601 timestamp}): stage={stage}; last_success={last_success}; first_failure={first_failure}; failure_type={failure_type} (task-worker diagnostic)`\n   \u2014 then return `STATUS: DONE` as its final line, per `subagent-contract`.\n2. **Completion detection reuses the poll-for-idle pattern already present in this same file**\n   (lines 120\u2013134, the session-health-check Mermaid flowchart: spawn \u2192 check verdict via\n   `ListAgents`/transcript-analyst \u2192 wait/re-check on a bounded schedule). Step 3's dispatch should\n   be followed by that same wait-for-idle check before proceeding \u2014 this is not a new\n   synchronization primitive, it is the existing one in this file, applied to the diagnostic\n   dispatch instead of invented fresh.\n3. **New step between 3 and 4**: after the wait confirms the diagnostic agent is idle, read the\n   result back \u2014 `backlog_view(selector=<item_ref/>, section=\"Context\", show=\"last\")` \u2014 and parse\n   `failure_type` from the most recent `DIAGNOSTIC` line. This value, not a return value from the\n   `Agent()` call, is what step 4's Mermaid tree (`Diag{\"failure_type?\"}`) branches on. The tree's\n   three branches (`interrupted` / `tool_error` / `unknown`) and the 2nd-failure \u2192 Escalation edge\n   are otherwise unchanged \u2014 this is a source-of-truth swap, not a logic change.\n\nThis makes the `\"2nd failure \u2192 Escalation\"` branch reachable with real data, closing feature-context\noutcome 3.\n\n---\n\n## 4. `skills/dispatch-contract/SKILL.md`\n\n**Current `<dispatch_selection>` text (line 11)**, verbatim:\n\n> A dispatch that hands over no dh operation \u2014 an independent review whose finding is its response \u2014\n> has nothing here to reach: any specialist that fits runs it, dh roster or not.\n\n**What this sentence actually governs** (confirmed by reading the whole 28-line file): which\n*agent* to select for a dispatch (any generalist vs. a `dh:` specialist/`dh:task-worker`) \u2014 not\nwhere output goes. The parenthetical \u2014 \"an independent review whose finding is its response\" \u2014 is\nthe sentence's *defining example* of \"hands over no dh operation,\" and that example is exactly the\nnow-broken pattern. `<artifact_consumption>` (lines 22\u201327) is orthogonal and correct as-is \u2014 no\nchange.\n\n**What still legitimately fits \"finding is its response\" today** (the two dispatch shapes in this\nplugin's own vocabulary where the dispatcher genuinely receives the result through an existing\nchannel):\n- `subagent_type: \"fork\"` \u2014 its result notification returns to the *same session*, on a later\n  turn; the finding does reach the dispatcher, just asynchronously.\n- A named `TeamCreate` teammate reporting via `SendMessage` to an addressable recipient (e.g.\n  `team-lead`) \u2014 the #3249/impact-analyst pattern, and the #3250 scope this item must not collapse\n  into (feature-context \u00a71.6, RT-ICA condition 7 APPROVED).\n\n**What must be excluded**: a bare, unnamed, non-team `Agent()` spawn \u2014 contract-verification's and\n`groom/error.md`'s diagnostic dispatch's exact shape. It has no return channel to the dispatcher at\nall, regardless of whether the dispatched task is \"pure analysis\" with no dh write in it. Per\n`.claude/rules/agent-output-contracts.md`: *\"That send is a notification, never the record.\"*\n\n**Required change**: qualify the sentence so it names the channel, not just the task shape. The\namended doctrine must convey, without changing `<artifact_consumption>`:\n\n1. \"Finding is its response\" is a valid category only for dispatch shapes with an actual channel\n   back to the dispatcher \u2014 name the two that qualify: `fork` (same-session notification) and a\n   named teammate addressed via `SendMessage`.\n2. A bare, unnamed, non-team `Agent()` spawn is excluded from that category unconditionally \u2014 such\n   a dispatch must always route its result through `backlog_groom` or `artifact_register` for a\n   later step to read back, even when the task itself performs no other dh operation.\n3. The \"any specialist that fits runs it, dh roster or not\" conclusion still holds *only* for\n   category-1 shapes; a bare-`Agent()` dispatch that must write to the backend has, by definition,\n   handed over a dh operation and therefore falls under this file's second paragraph (dispatch a\n   `dh:` specialist or `dh:task-worker`) \u2014 which is already true of `dh:contract-verification` and\n   `dh:task-worker`, so no agent selection actually changes; only the doctrine's description of\n   *why* changes.\n\n---\n\n## 5. `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md`\n\n**Grooming hypothesis confirmed \u2014 no change required.**\n\n`<result_destination>` (lines 23\u201337) already states the correct general fallback: *\"every other\ndocument is an artifact, registered with `artifact_register`\u2026 Hand the next step a plan, task, or\nartifact id.\"* `contract-verification.md`'s line-151 override was the thing opting *out* of this\nalready-correct fallback; \u00a71 removes that override rather than the fallback needing to change.\n\nOne confirmed nuance, not a required edit: `<status>`'s convention (*\"Begin your response with\n`STATUS: DONE`\u2026 Consumers branch on that line\"*) does not itself claim synchronous delivery, and\nnone is required of it \u2014 under a bare non-team `Agent()` dispatch, nothing reads that line at all\n(the same fact this whole item is about), and that is fine, because `<result_destination>` was\nalready designed to be the load-bearing channel and `<status>` the best-effort one. This matches\n`.claude/rules/agent-output-contracts.md`'s explicit framing of the STATUS/SendMessage send as *\"a\nnotification, never the record.\"* No file needs to say this more explicitly than it already does;\nnoted here only to confirm the hypothesis was checked, not assumed.\n\n---\n\n## Call-flow summary (post-fix)\n\n```\nimplement-feature step 4a\n  \u251c\u2500 dispatch Agent(dh:contract-verification, {..., issue}) \u2014 fire-and-forget, no branch on return\n  \u2502     \u2514\u2500 contract-verification.md itself: extract contracts \u2192 compare \u2192 backlog_groom(\n  \u2502           selector=\"#{issue}\", section=\"Concerns\", append=True,\n  \u2502           content=\"- [ ] CONTRACT: ...\" | \"- [x] CONTRACT: No contract concerns ...\")\n  \u2502        \u2192 STATUS: DONE (best-effort terminal text, not load-bearing)\n  \u251c\u2500 Per-task Confirmation Gate: backlog_view(section=\"Concerns\", show=\"last\") \u2014 fresh read\n  \u2514\u2500 /complete-implementation validation stage: reads full Concerns section (unchanged) \u2014\n       backstop that guarantees eventual delivery even if the gate read raced the write\n\ngroom/error.md Agent Failure\n  \u251c\u2500 dispatch Agent(dh:task-worker, \"...and backlog_groom(Context, DIAGNOSTIC ...)\")\n  \u251c\u2500 wait-for-idle (reuses existing lines 120-134 poll pattern)\n  \u251c\u2500 backlog_view(selector=<item_ref/>, section=\"Context\", show=\"last\") \u2192 parse failure_type\n  \u2514\u2500 Mermaid tree branches on the parsed value (logic unchanged)\n\ndispatch-contract/SKILL.md doctrine\n  \u2514\u2500 \"finding is its response\" scoped to {fork, named-teammate+SendMessage} only;\n     bare non-team Agent() always routes through backlog_groom/artifact_register\n\nsubagent-contract/SKILL.md\n  \u2514\u2500 unchanged; its artifact_register fallback was already the correct general rule\n```\n\n## Verification note for implementers (not in scope for this spec to resolve)\n\n`Context` is assumed to be a standard section on any groomed backlog item (confirmed present on\n#3276 via its own `sections_index`). If a future item lacks it, `backlog_groom(..., append=True)`\nis used identically to how `Concerns` is already appended elsewhere in this plugin without an\nexistence check \u2014 this spec assumes, consistent with existing usage, that the tool creates the\nsection if absent.\n"}